<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Devon is a county of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> with coasts on both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel. Its name comes from the Dumnonii, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> people of the peninsula, through the post-Roman kingdom of Dumnonia. The area was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in the 7th and 8th centuries, and its county town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devon.jpg" alt="Devon" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Theasby · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dartmoor_ponies_by_the_B3212_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4546147.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
